A||||12||william_thorniii||Aluminum Headlight Gears (84-86) from Chris Cook||||5||
Z||000000||william_thorniii||10-26-2000||12:28 PM||||Friends,<P>&lt;Sorry for the typically long post&gt;<P>I purchased a set of aluminum headlight gears from one Mr. Christopher Cook last winter.  The prospect of never having my 85 GT's lights fail again was well worth the price of $35 per gear.  I didn't understand how an aluminum gear would work in a resistance stop motor, but assumed that a proprietor had at least done preliminary testing of his own product beyond a fit test.  <P>Upon getting my new motors back together, my fears were confirmed.  The lights shoot up quickly but now hit a positive mechanical stop from the end of linkage travel.  They hit this positive stop at full speed and therefore get "jammed".  If one tries to power them down, the relays will just get burnt out.  <P>Has anyone else experienced this problem?  If not, does anyone have a set of Alan Mooty's aluminum headlight gears?  How do they incorporate cushion at the end of travel?<P>My proposal is this, please give me any and all feedback...  I intend to bore out the holes that are currently press-fit over the sprocket fingers (stamped sheet-metal gear that press fits to main shaft of motor to drive plastic gear via green dampers in stock set-up) and place a compliant material bushing between the aluminum and the sheet metal.  My question is this... what material would best suit itself to this application.  The original materials breakdown from being submersed in grease is what caused all of the failures in the headlight assemblies in the first place.  Any plastics/materials/chemical engineers in the house?  <P>Otherwise are there any other ideas for providing a cushion at the end of travel?  Or does anyone have current contact info on Chris Cook? <P>Many Thanks,<P>William T. Thorn, III<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Rodney||10-26-2000||03:40 PM||||One option is to install new OEM parts. They have been proven to work.  : )<BR> <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>www.rodneydickman.com</A> <P>Rodney||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||10-26-2000||04:03 PM||||Rodney,<P>Don't make me yell at you about SPAM... j/k :~p<P>If you will recall, I was one of the folks that talked extensively with you about this very subject BEFORE you had the new gears made.  In retrospect I should have gone that way, but the lure of NEVER having to replace any internal headlight component made me drunk with stupidity.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-26-2000||07:28 PM||||When you figure that it took years for the original bumpers to quit I would't worry much about them. If you can fit them in then they are probably the easiest bet. I'm told some people have used RTV silicone. I don't know just how they do it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||007DOUG||10-26-2000||08:18 PM||||Bore holes like this:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.euronet.nl/users/fo_elmo/Fieroproduction.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.euronet.nl/users/fo_elmo/Fieroproduction.htm</A>  <P>I agree with Ogre. It took 12+ years for the originals to wear out under daily use. Alot of us use our fieros for "pleasure" now. Rebuilding them with factory parts should last forever. <P>Spamming begins below this line. read at your own risk.<BR>-----------------------------------------------------------<BR>I've sold the 84-86 headlight rebuilt kits with a metal alloy gear for years.<P>I've never had a problem with any motor jamming. Those bored holes replace the need for the green bumper stops that wear-out.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||10-26-2000||08:24 PM||||Umm.... I think doug ment this link <A HREF="http://www.euronet.nl/users/fo_elmo/headlightact.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.euronet.nl/users/fo_elmo/headlightact.htm</A>  but I don't see what holes he ment.||||reg||1
Z||000006||007DOUG||10-26-2000||08:27 PM||||OK, lets try this again.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rebuildkit(1)_1.jpg"> <P>P.S. Cliff, I can't edit my post. Says invalid password?||||reg||1
Z||000007||william_thorniii||10-27-2000||08:24 AM||||theogre,<P>Now that I realized just exactly how much more trouble it was to go this route... belive me I would go with another factory set-up.  Remember though at the time Rodney was not yet making them and did not want to pay for Ecklers of GM original (eeek!).  Unfortunately with this design, there is no allowance for the stock green bushing material.  <P>007DOUG,<P>That is exactly the same as the gears that I have in there now.  All dimensions even look correct.  When mine spin, they spin so fast that at the mechanical limits of travel, they get jammed.  I can feel a significant difference in tension when cranking the motor by hand.  <P>I have so many questions....<P>You haven't ever had this problem?  <P>Is there anything else that needs to be modified during the installation?  <P>How can a resistance shut-off motor have a positive mechanical stop?  <P>Has the lifting mechanism been retrofitted with a rubber bumper thereby stopping travel before binding? <P>Thanks for the picture.  Those were the holes that I was considering boring out to install a (rubber hose) bushing.  My original idea was that it would provide enough cushion to prevent the motor from binding the lifting linkage.  <P>Many Thanks,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000008||007DOUG||10-27-2000||11:37 AM||||I've sold about 50 of thses kits, most rebuilt by the customer, a few by myself. Never had a problem like that. The only problems I've had was misdiagnosis. A switch was needed rather than a gear.<P>Your switch isn't sticking is it?<P>Nothing needs to be modified. Its a direct swap.<P>My only other suggestion would be bent "forks". Since the motor works freely, you've obviously assemled it correctly.<P>Send me the motors and let me look at them.||||reg||1
Z||000009||bHooper||10-27-2000||11:53 AM||||Doug... who could that be?  Still haven't had a chance to order a new switch.  <P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000010||william_thorniii||10-27-2000||11:57 AM||||007DOUG,<P>I appreciate the offer, but I am just going to buy a set of Rodney's gears.  The light assemblies were working fine before I decided to go to aluminum.  I went this way because I was noticing some symptoms that they were going to fail.  Since the swap, I have replaced both of the relays just to make sure.  <P>I don't know what exactly is my problem, but can assure you that after these things run up or down, that they bind.  I don't know what causes the bind, but I do know that to my hand the resistance felt during the initial "binding period" is substantially greater than that during the normal travel of the lamp.  If you have another diagnosis, please feel free to let me know.  These are on the 85 GT I am preparing for sale.  I do have another Fiero that could use these if I could figure out what is causing the problems.  <P>Rodney,<P>I am looking forward to installing your gears and getting rid of the "snobbish" i.e. stuck-up headlights.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
Z||000011||007DOUG||10-27-2000||11:11 PM||||I'd say its the switch.<P>Remove the motor from the headlight "housing", but leave all wires plug to it. Hold the motor in your hand and turn your headlights on. The motor will run forever. Theres no resistance to the switch thus the motor won't shut off.<P>Now turn your headlights off. The motor will run in the opposite direction forever.<P>The switch tells the motor when to stop. Your switch isn't doing that. Its binding at the top of travel only becuase it doesn't know to shut off.||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||10-28-2000||02:18 PM||||Bill, I have to agree with Doug on that one. The problem is most likely the limit switch on the motor having a problem. The relay wouldn't do this I don't think. It's likely the limit switch is worn so it takes more torque than normal to shut off the motor. That would definalty bind them at one or both ends of the travel. ((could posibly be too much play or wear on the little block on the motor shaft that activates the switch.))<P>If there's any play in those holes you could center the plate in the holes and then squirt RTV in around the ears to pad them. Just trim off any excess that sticks out with a razor blade.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-28-2000).]||||reg||1
